The clause covers one situation: a helper answers after the report is already filed. None of the candidate triggers describe that situation, so every answer is false.

Several candidates touch on helpers (dispatched, died, named in the routing table, dispatched twice), but none is a helper answering after the report is filed. The approval, session, invoice, supplier, carrier and shipment candidates are unrelated.

{"answers":[false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false]}

The clause's only trigger is that a helper agent has fallen (died). Only the question about a helper agent that died matches it.

Questions about shipment lateness, session state, invoice work, approvals, suppliers and carrier answers describe other situations. The questions about a helper agent being dispatched, being named in the routing table, or being dispatched twice are also different situations from a helper agent dying.

{"answers":[false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,true,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false]}

The clause says: do not send the draft while the user's answer is still outstanding. The only trigger it specifies is an approval or answer that is still pending. Only the question about a person whose approval is needed not having answered matches that. The user's answer is outstanding, so it is close enough to "a person whose approval is needed has not answered".

The explicit yes question describes the opposite situation, so it does not match. All the other questions concern unrelated topics.

{"answers":[false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,true,false,false,false,false,false,false,false]}

The clause says to hold the weekly report until the reviewer replies. The only candidate that matches is the approval one: a person whose approval is needed has not answered. The reviewer is a person who hasn't replied, which fits "has not answered." The other approval candidate, an explicit yes, is the opposite case.

All other candidates (shipment lateness, session state, invoice work, helper agents, suppliers, carrier answers) are not mentioned in the clause.

{"answers":[false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,true,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false]}

The clause says: "I just restarted the session? Then re-read the rules before you act." The only trigger it specifies is that the session was just restarted.

Only the question about a restart matches. The compaction and fork questions describe different session states, so they do not match. None of the shipment, invoice, helper agent, approval, supplier or carrier questions are covered.

{"answers":[false,false,false,false,false,false,false,true,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false,false]}

The clause only covers an unlisted supplier, meaning one not in the language table. Only the question about a supplier not in the language table matches that trigger. A listed supplier, a supplier with two languages, and every other topic (shipments, sessions, invoices, helper agents, approvals, carrier replies) are not specified.

